k controller... The calculating/indicating device type MultiLoad II from Toptech Systems.\n\nSince this is a fragment, I should extract what's present:\n\n- certificate: number \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\", revision 0, project_number \"2638810\", page_total 15, member_state \"The Netherlands\"\n\nWait — the certificate number is \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\" and revision 0. The number field: \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\". The revision_history: revision \"0\".\n\n- recommendation: id \"R117\", edition... The R 117/2007 refers to OIML R117 edition 2007? Actually R117 \"Measuring systems for liquids other than water\" — the 2007 edition. The certificate number format \"R 117/2007-A\" means recommendation R117, edition 2007? Actually the \"2007\" here might be the year of edition. Hmm, \"R 117/2007\" — OIML R 117 was published in 1995 and revised in 2007. So edition 2007? But schema says edition (int). I could put edition: 2007? Actually careful: \"R 117/2007\" is the way R117 certs are numbered — the 2007 refers to the edition year of the recommendation. I'll put edition: 2007? Hmm, but I'm not 100% sure. The certificate number \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\" — \"R 117/2007\" indicates the recommendation and its edition year (2007). Scheme: \"A\"? The \"-A-\" in the number could indicate scheme A. Hmm. For R117, certificates are issued under OIML CS with scheme A or B. The certificate number format: \"OIML certificate number R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\". The \"A\" after \"R 117/2007\" is the scheme. So scheme: \"A\".\n\nActually let me be careful — the certificate.scheme field is \"(A|B)\". In the number \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\", yes the A is the scheme. And revision 0.\n\n- Components: The calculating/indicating device type MultiLoad II from Toptech Systems. Since this fragment mentions \"Part: — Calculating/indicating device\", this is a component of the certified measuring system. So components[] entry:\n  - role: \"calculating/indicating device\"\n  - type_designations: [\"MultiLoad II\"]\n  - manufacturer: Toptech Systems — but components schema has role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. Hmm, there's no manufacturer field in components. But rule 6 says record each as components[] entry with role, type_designations, and any sub-characteristics. I could add \"manufacturer\": \"Toptech Systems\" as an extra field — schema says don't fabricate, but adding manufacturer seems reasonable... The schema is layered; components have role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I might include the producer info. I think it's safe to add \"manufacturer\" field to the component since it's present in the markdown. But strictly the schema doesn't have it. Hmm. The rules say \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it\" — that's about not fabricating. Adding manufacturer to component is present in markdown. I'll include it as it's valuable.\n\n- The first table (top of fragment) belongs to a previous part (probably the measuring/transmitter part from part 3). Since we don't know which component it belongs to (the fragment starts mid-certificate), I could put it as... Hmm. The table has Environmental classes M3/E3/H3, Accuracy class 0,3/0,5/1,0/1,5/2,5, Ambient temperature range -25°C/+55°C or -40°C/+55°C. This is likely Table for the \"Part: measuring device / transmitter\" or maybe the whole system. Given the page before (page 10) probably described a different part. Since we can't be sure, maybe I should include it as a matrix_table or config_level characteristics with a note.\n\nActually, given it's a fragment, I should capture the content present. Options:\n1. Put it under matrix_tables as \"General characteristics (part unspecified)\".\n2. Put under characteristics.config_level for ambient temperature with condition.\n\nThe accuracy class 0,3 / 0,5 / 1,0 / 1,5 / 2,5 — these are multiple values; for R117 accuracy classes are 0,15...2,5 (for fuel dispensers). Could be the system-level accuracy classes. Hmm, this first table could be general characteristics of the whole measuring system (from page 10). Since the fragment doesn't say which part, I'll include it as a matrix_table with description noting it's likely from the preceding page (unspecified part). Actually, maybe safer: put in characteristics.type_level? Accuracy class has 5 values — that's a list, per rules \"Lists of values\" should be model matrix. But it's really \"the cert covers these accuracy classes\". The recommendation.accuracy_classes field exists! I could put accuracy_classes: [0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.5] in recommendation. Hmm but that's for the first table which may be a component. The calculating/indicating device has accuracy class 0,5. Hmm.\n\nActually for R117 measuring systems, accuracy classes are 0.15, 0.2, 0.25, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.5 (for fuel dispensers etc.). The classes 0.3/0.5/1.0/1.5/2.5 fit the whole system. The environmental classes M3/E3/H3 fit the whole system too (mechanical M3, electromagnetic E3, humidity H3 per R117). Actually M1/M2/M3 are mechanical environment classes, E1-E4 electromagnetic... wait no, in R117: E1-E4 are \"disturbance\" severity levels, H1-H3 humidity classes. So M3/E3/H3 = system level. So the first table is likely \"General characteristics of the measuring system\" (whole system). But it might also be the measuring device part. Environmental class M3/E2 for the calculating device (E2 is lower). The first table has E3 which is higher — consistent with a different component or the system.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, I'll include it as a matrix_tables entry named something like \"General characteristics (continuation from previous page — part unspecified)\" with rows. Or put ambient temperature in config_level with axis. Let me think about what's most useful.\n\nI'll do:\n- matrix_tables: one entry for the first table:\n  - name: \"general_characteristics_unspecified_part\" — hmm, name should be descriptive. \"General characteristics (previous page, part unspecif