pe SMP\" — these suggest MultiLoad II variants: EXL, DIV-2, SMP. These are type variants of the calculating/indicating device? \"for type EXL and type DIV-2\" — MultiLoad II EXL and MultiLoad II DIV-2. And SMP. These could be model variants! Should I add model_family with models EXL, DIV-2, SMP? Hmm. The CID type is MultiLoad II; software sets validated per type variant EXL, DIV-2, SMP. It's plausible these are different MultiLoad II variants. But without more context, I could mention in matrix_tables descriptions: \"Validated software versions with checksums for type EXL and type DIV-2 (MultiLoad II)\". I think that's safer than inventing a model family. Actually the cert type designations likely include MultiLoad II EXL, MultiLoad II DIV-2, MultiLoad II SMP elsewhere. For this fragment, I'll note in the component's characteristics or keep in matrix table names/descriptions.\n\nHmm, should certified_type.type_designations include \"MultiLoad II\"? Yes — the fragment mentions it. And \"EXL\", \"DIV-2\", \"SMP\" as variants mentioned. I'll include type_designations: [\"MultiLoad II\"] and maybe note variants. The schema: certified_type.type_designations[] — all type/model identifiers mentioned. So [\"MultiLoad II\"] plus maybe [\"MultiLoad II EXL\", \"MultiLoad II DIV-2\", \"MultiLoad II SMP\"]? The text says \"type EXL and type DIV-2\" and \"type SMP\" — these are type identifiers within MultiLoad II family. I'll include them as type designations? Risky. I'll add them as separate entries since the text explicitly names them as types: \"MultiLoad II EXL\", \"MultiLoad II DIV-2\", \"MultiLoad II SMP\" — hmm, that constructs names not verbatim. Alternatively include \"EXL\", \"DIV-2\", \"SMP\". I think it's cleaner to keep type_designations: [\"MultiLoad II\"] and put the EXL/DIV-2/SMP references in the matrix table descriptions. Or model_family with models EXL, DIV-2, SMP for the CID. Given rule 4 (model detection) — this is software differing by type, not necessarily