ient temperature: \"-25 °C / +55 °C\" → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C.\n\n2. The first table's part — unknown name. Since it's a fragment continuation, maybe attach to a component with role from previous fragment unknown. I could add a matrix_tables entry for it and NOT create a component. That's cleanest.\n\nAlso the transmitter software — the transmitter is a component (from previous fragment, likely \"Part: — Measuring device\" or \"Transmitter\"). I won't create a transmitter component since its name isn't in this fragment; software tables go to matrix_tables with names.\n\nHmm, but wait — should the transmitter software tables be under components? The heading on page 11 is just \"The validated software versions and checksums are:\" — belonging to the part described on page 10 (previous fragment). Since we don't know the part, matrix_tables is fine.\n\nNow, config_level for the ambient temperature of the first table: \"-25 °C / +55 °C\" standard, \"-40 °C / +55 °C in case the measurement results can be read and/or recorded with an approved instrument\". This is a configuration condition! config_level: attribute ambient_temperature_range, axis/condition... config_level values: {condition, value}. So:\n\ncharacteristics.config_level: [\n  {\n    attribute: \"ambient_temperature_range\",\n    axis: \"reading_of_measurement_results\",\n    values: [\n      {condition: \"standard\", value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"},\n      {condition: \"measurement results can be read and/or recorded with an approved instrument\", value: {min: -40, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nBut whose characteristics? This is type_level of the unknown part. Since I'm putting the first table in matrix_tables, maybe skip config_level to avoid mixing. Hmm. The rules say use config_level when attributes vary by configuration. This ambient temp condition is exactly that. But the fragment doesn't tell us which part this table belongs to. I'll put it in matrix_tables row